// New articles, 15 November 2026 (part 2): controversial topics in society, the internet and belief.
ADDART({id:"darkweb",t:"The Dark Web",c:"The Modern World",i:"🧅",w:"Dark web",r:["internet","cybersecurity","crypto","cryptocurrency","dataprivacy","hacking"],
p:[["A hidden part of the internet","The dark web is made of websites that need special software to reach."],["Privacy and crime","It protects dissidents and journalists, but also hosts illegal markets."],["Not the deep web","The ‘deep web’ simply means pages search engines don’t list, such as email."]],
l:"The dark web is the part of the internet made up of websites that cannot be reached with an ordinary browser and are hidden from search engines. The best-known dark web network is Tor, short for The Onion Router, which sends traffic through several volunteer-run computers around the world, wrapping it in layers of encryption like the layers of an onion, so that neither the website nor anyone watching can easily tell who is visiting. Dark web sites use addresses ending in ‘.onion’. The dark web is often confused with the deep web, which is simply any page not indexed by search engines, such as online banking, email and private databases, and is far larger than the public web. The dark web is used by journalists, whistleblowers and people living under censorship, but it is also known for illegal marketplaces selling drugs, stolen data and weapons, which police around the world work to shut down.",
s:[["How it works","Onion routing was developed in the mid-1990s by researchers at the US Naval Research Laboratory to protect government communications. The Tor software was released to the public in 2002, and the non-profit Tor Project was founded in 2006. Each connection passes through three relays; each knows only the step before and after it. This makes tracking users much harder, though not impossible."],["Legitimate uses","People in countries with heavy censorship use Tor to read blocked news and talk safely. News organizations, including the BBC and The New York Times, run .onion versions of their sites, and many offer SecureDrop, a system that lets sources send documents anonymously. Facebook launched an onion address in 2014."],["Crime and policing","The marketplace Silk Road, opened in 2011, sold illegal drugs paid for in Bitcoin. The FBI shut it down in 2013, and its founder Ross Ulbricht was sentenced to life in prison in 2015; he was pardoned by US President Donald Trump in January 2025. Later markets such as AlphaBay (closed in 2017) and Hydra (closed in 2022) were taken down by international police operations. Stolen passwords and credit card details are also traded on the dark web."],["Risks","Visiting the dark web is legal in most countries, but buying illegal goods is not. Many sites are scams, carry malware or contain disturbing material. Security experts advise ordinary users that they gain little from it and that the privacy it offers depends on careful use."]],
k:[["Main network","Tor (The Onion Router)"],["Address ending","‘.onion’"],["Origin","US Naval Research Laboratory, mid-1990s"],["Tor released","2002"],["Famous market shut down","Silk Road, 2013"],["Not the same as","The deep web"]],
d:[["1995","Onion routing research begins"],["2002","Tor is released"],["2006","The Tor Project is founded"],["2011","Silk Road opens"],["2013","FBI shuts down Silk Road"],["2014","Facebook launches an onion site"],["2017","AlphaBay is taken down"],["2022","Hydra, the largest market, is shut down"]],
s2:[["Surface, deep and dark","People often picture the internet as an iceberg. The surface web is what search engines find. The deep web, below it, holds private pages such as your email inbox, medical records and school portals. The dark web is a very small part of the deep web that is deliberately hidden."],["Privacy debate","Some governments argue that anonymity tools shield criminals and should be restricted. Privacy groups reply that the same tools protect human rights workers, abuse survivors and journalists, and that weakening them would make everyone less safe."]],
sp:["The onion symbol","Tor’s logo is an onion because data is wrapped in layers of encryption. Each relay peels off one layer, like peeling an onion, to learn where to send the data next. Only the last relay sees the destination, and only the first sees who sent it."],
g:[["Encryption","Scrambling data so only the intended reader can understand it"],["Anonymity","Being unknown or unidentified"],["Relay","A computer that passes data along a network"],["Whistleblower","A person who exposes wrongdoing inside an organization"]],
f:["Tor was created with US government funding.","The deep web is much bigger than the surface web.","Dark web addresses are long strings of random-looking letters ending in .onion."],
fun:"Some dark web markets copied the look of ordinary shopping sites, with star ratings and customer reviews for sellers.",
tl:"Ang dark web ay bahagi ng internet na naaabot lamang gamit ang espesyal na software tulad ng Tor. Ginagamit ito ng mga mamamahayag at ng mga taong nasa ilalim ng sensura, ngunit mayroon din itong mga ilegal na pamilihan na sinisikap isara ng pulisya."});

ADDART({id:"conspiracy",t:"Conspiracy Theories",c:"Society",i:"🕵️",w:"Conspiracy theory",r:["misinformation","scimeth","antisemitism","ufos","secretsocieties","propaganda"],
p:[["Secret plots","A conspiracy theory claims that powerful groups are secretly behind events."],["Why people believe","They offer simple explanations and a sense of hidden knowledge."],["Real and imagined","Some real conspiracies have happened, but most theories lack evidence."]],
l:"A conspiracy theory is an explanation of an event or situation that claims it was secretly caused by a powerful group acting with hidden, usually harmful, purposes, when other explanations are better supported by evidence. Famous examples include claims that the 1969 Moon landing was faked, that the Earth is flat, that the assassination of US President John F. Kennedy was a government plot, that aircraft spray chemicals from the sky (‘chemtrails’) and that vaccines or 5G phone masts are part of a plan to harm people. Conspiracy theories are often impossible to disprove, because any evidence against them can be explained as part of the cover-up. Some real conspiracies have been exposed, such as the Watergate scandal, which is one reason people find such theories believable. Psychologists study why conspiracy theories spread, especially during times of fear and uncertainty, and how they can lead to distrust, prejudice and even violence.",
s:[["Why they appeal","Humans naturally look for patterns and causes. Big events, such as a president’s death or a pandemic, can feel as if they need big causes. Conspiracy theories can make people feel they understand what others do not, give someone to blame and offer a sense of belonging to a group of ‘truth-seekers’."],["Real conspiracies","Watergate (1972–74), in which officials working for President Richard Nixon broke into opposition offices and covered it up, was a real conspiracy uncovered by journalists. Other proven cases include the CIA’s secret MKUltra mind-control experiments and the Tuskegee study, in which US health officials left Black men with syphilis untreated from 1932 to 1972. These were revealed through documents, investigations and whistleblowers, which is how real conspiracies are usually exposed."],["Harm","Conspiracy theories have fuelled prejudice, especially antisemitic myths of secret Jewish control. During the COVID-19 pandemic, false claims about 5G led to phone masts being set on fire in Britain, and vaccine conspiracy theories reduced vaccination. The QAnon movement, which began online in 2017, was linked to violent incidents in the United States."],["Testing a claim","Useful questions include: What is the evidence, and who provides it? Could many people really keep the secret? Does the theory explain away all contrary evidence? Is there a simpler explanation? Fact-checkers, scientists and historians use these methods, and people who are open to changing their minds tend to resist false theories."]],
k:[["Definition","A claim that hidden powerful groups secretly caused events"],["Famous examples","Faked Moon landing, flat Earth, chemtrails"],["Real conspiracy","Watergate (1972–74)"],["Spread by","Word of mouth, books, social media"],["Countered by","Evidence, critical thinking, fact-checking"]],
d:[["1903","The forged Protocols of the Elders of Zion appears"],["1963","Kennedy assassination sparks many theories"],["1972–74","Watergate, a real conspiracy, is exposed"],["1976","A book claims the Moon landings were faked"],["2017","QAnon begins online"],["2020","5G and COVID-19 rumours lead to mast attacks"]],
s2:[["Debunking the Moon hoax","Astronauts left mirrors on the Moon that scientists still bounce lasers off to measure its distance. Hundreds of kilograms of Moon rock have been studied worldwide, and other countries, including the Soviet Union, tracked the missions. Space probes have photographed the landing sites, showing equipment and astronauts’ tracks."],["Thousands keeping a secret","A 2016 study estimated how long large secrets could last before someone revealed them. It suggested that a plot involving thousands of people, such as faking the Moon landings, would very likely have been exposed within a few years."]],
sp:["The flat Earth","Ancient Greek scholars knew the Earth was round by about 500 BC, and Eratosthenes measured its size around 240 BC. The idea that medieval people all believed in a flat Earth is itself a myth. Modern flat-Earth groups grew online in the 2010s despite photographs from space and countless experiments."],
g:[["Conspiracy","A secret plan by a group to do something harmful or illegal"],["Evidence","Facts or information showing whether something is true"],["Cover-up","An attempt to hide wrongdoing"],["Critical thinking","Carefully judging claims and evidence"]],
f:["Watergate was a real conspiracy exposed by newspaper reporters.","Mirrors left on the Moon by Apollo astronauts are still used for experiments.","People knew the Earth was round more than 2,000 years ago."],
fun:"A joke conspiracy theory, ‘Birds Aren’t Real’, which claims birds are government drones, was created in 2017 to make fun of misinformation.",
tl:"Ang conspiracy theory ay paliwanag na nagsasabing lihim na pakana ng makapangyarihang grupo ang isang pangyayari, kahit may mas matibay na ebidensya para sa ibang paliwanag. May mga totoong sabwatan tulad ng Watergate, ngunit karamihan sa mga teorya ay walang batayan at maaaring magdulot ng pinsala."});

ADDART({id:"secretsocieties",t:"Secret Societies",c:"History",i:"🔺",w:"Secret society",r:["conspiracy","bonifacio","phrev","relig","enlightenment"],
p:[["Members only","Secret societies are groups that keep their rituals or membership hidden."],["Freemasons and more","Famous examples include the Freemasons, Rosicrucians and the Bavarian Illuminati."],["Revolutionary groups","Some, like the Katipunan, plotted revolutions in secret."]],
l:"A secret society is an organization whose members keep some of its activities, rituals, symbols or even its existence hidden from outsiders. Some are fraternal clubs with private ceremonies, such as the Freemasons; others were political groups that had to work in secret to avoid arrest, such as the Carbonari in 19th-century Italy and the Katipunan in the Philippines; and some were criminal, such as the Ku Klux Klan in the United States or certain Chinese triads. Because of their secrecy, they have long inspired rumours and conspiracy theories, especially about the Freemasons and the Illuminati, a short-lived Bavarian society that was banned in 1785 but is still imagined by some to secretly rule the world. Historians study secret societies through their surviving documents, which often reveal ordinary concerns such as charity, friendship, self-improvement and politics.",
s:[["The Freemasons","Freemasonry grew from guilds of medieval stonemasons. The first Grand Lodge was founded in London in 1717. Masons meet in lodges, use symbols such as the square and compasses and progress through degrees with private rituals. Famous Freemasons included George Washington, Mozart and many Filipino revolutionaries such as José Rizal. The Catholic Church has banned its members from joining since 1738."],["The Illuminati","The Order of the Illuminati was founded in Bavaria on 1 May 1776 by the law professor Adam Weishaupt to spread Enlightenment ideas and oppose religious and royal control of public life. It never had more than about 2,000 members and was banned by the Bavarian government in 1785. After the French Revolution, writers falsely blamed it for the uprising, beginning a myth that survives today."],["Revolutionary societies","Secret societies were common among those plotting against kings or colonial rulers. The Carbonari worked for Italian unity and freedom. In China, groups such as the Heaven and Earth Society opposed the Qing dynasty. The Katipunan, founded in Manila in 1892, used passwords, oaths signed in blood and code names before launching the Philippine Revolution in 1896."],["University and fraternal societies","Skull and Bones, founded at Yale University in 1832, admits a small number of students each year and has included presidents and senators. Many fraternities and clubs also keep traditions secret, which critics say can hide bullying or favoritism."]],
k:[["Oldest Masonic Grand Lodge","London, 1717"],["Illuminati founded","1 May 1776, Bavaria"],["Illuminati banned","1785"],["Katipunan founded","7 July 1892"],["Skull and Bones founded","1832, Yale University"]],
d:[["1614","Rosicrucian manifestos appear in Germany"],["1717","First Masonic Grand Lodge in London"],["1776","Illuminati founded in Bavaria"],["1785","Illuminati banned"],["1832","Skull and Bones founded at Yale"],["1865","Ku Klux Klan formed"],["1892","Katipunan founded in Manila"]],
s2:[["The eye in the pyramid","The ‘eye of providence’ above an unfinished pyramid on the back of the US one-dollar bill is often claimed to be an Illuminati symbol. It comes from the Great Seal of the United States, designed in 1782, and was a common Christian symbol of God watching over people; it was not used by the Illuminati."],["Why secrecy?","Groups keep secrets for different reasons: to protect members from persecution, to make rituals feel special, to build loyalty, or to hide illegal activity. Historians note that secrecy itself makes outsiders suspicious, regardless of what the group actually does."]],
sp:["The Rosicrucians","In 1614–1616, pamphlets appeared in Germany describing a secret brotherhood founded by a mysterious Christian Rosenkreuz, who held hidden wisdom about nature and the spirit. No such brotherhood seems to have existed at the time, but the story inspired later groups that took the Rosicrucian name."],
g:[["Lodge","A local branch or meeting place of Freemasons"],["Ritual","A ceremony performed in a set way"],["Fraternal","Like brothers; based on friendship and mutual help"],["Oath","A solemn promise"]],
f:["The Bavarian Illuminati lasted only about nine years.","José Rizal and Emilio Aguinaldo were Freemasons.","The ‘all-seeing eye’ on the dollar bill is not an Illuminati symbol."],
fun:"Katipunan members used secret passwords, and new recruits had to answer questions in a blindfolded initiation.",
tl:"Ang lihim na samahan ay organisasyong itinatago ang ilang gawain, ritwal o kasapian nito. Kabilang sa mga kilala ang mga Freemason, ang Illuminati na ipinagbawal noong 1785, at ang Katipunan, na lihim na nagplano ng Rebolusyong Pilipino."});

ADDART({id:"ufos",t:"UFOs and Unexplained Sightings",c:"Science",i:"🛸",w:"Unidentified flying object",r:["exoplanet","scimeth","conspiracy","radioastronomy","illusions"],
p:[["Unidentified objects","A UFO is anything seen in the sky that cannot be identified at first."],["Mostly explained","Most turn out to be planes, balloons, satellites, planets or tricks of light."],["Serious study","Governments now investigate unexplained sightings for safety and security."]],
l:"An unidentified flying object, or UFO, is anything seen in the sky that an observer cannot identify. In recent years, governments have used the term unidentified anomalous phenomena, or UAP. The modern UFO craze began in June 1947, when the American pilot Kenneth Arnold reported nine shining objects over Washington State moving ‘like a saucer skipping across water’, which newspapers turned into ‘flying saucers’. Weeks later, debris found near Roswell, New Mexico, began one of the most famous UFO stories; the US Air Force later explained it as a secret balloon project. Investigations have shown that most sightings are aircraft, weather balloons, satellites, bright planets such as Venus, drones, unusual clouds or camera effects. A small number remain unexplained, usually because of too little information. Scientists say there is no confirmed evidence that any UFO is an alien spacecraft, while the search for life beyond Earth continues through astronomy.",
s:[["Flying saucers","After Kenneth Arnold’s report on 24 June 1947, hundreds of sightings followed. In July 1947, a ranch worker found debris near Roswell, and an army press release at first said a ‘flying disc’ had been recovered before calling it a weather balloon. In 1994 the Air Force said it came from Project Mogul, a secret program using balloons to detect Soviet nuclear tests."],["Government studies","From 1952 to 1969, the US Air Force’s Project Blue Book studied 12,618 reports, leaving 701 unidentified. In 2017 newspapers revealed a secret Pentagon program, and in 2020 the US Navy released videos of objects filmed by fighter pilots. In 2022 the Pentagon created an office called AARO; its 2024 historical report found no evidence that the government had found alien technology."],["Explaining sightings","Many reports come from Venus, which can look dazzlingly bright near the horizon, or from satellite chains such as Starlink, which appear as lines of moving lights. Balloons, kites, lanterns, birds and drones are common causes. Cameras can create illusions through reflections, lens flares and parallax, which can make slow objects seem to move fast."],["Life in the universe","Astronomers search for life using radio telescopes in the SETI program and by studying the atmospheres of planets around other stars. In 2023 a NASA panel on UAP called for better data and scientific study. The Fermi paradox asks why, if life is common in the universe, we have found no sign of it."]],
k:[["Stands for","Unidentified flying object"],["Newer term","UAP, unidentified anomalous phenomena"],["‘Flying saucer’ sighting","Kenneth Arnold, 24 June 1947"],["Roswell incident","July 1947"],["Project Blue Book","1952–1969, 12,618 reports"],["Evidence of aliens","None confirmed"]],
d:[["1947","Kenneth Arnold’s sighting; Roswell debris found"],["1952","Project Blue Book begins"],["1961","Frank Drake writes his equation for estimating alien civilizations"],["1969","Project Blue Book closes"],["1994","Air Force explains Roswell as Project Mogul"],["2020","US Navy videos released"],["2022","Pentagon creates AARO"],["2023","NASA publishes its UAP study"]],
s2:[["Why people see UFOs","Our brains are not good at judging the size, distance and speed of a light in the sky with no landmarks for comparison. A small, close object and a large, distant one can look the same. Excitement and expectation can also shape what people believe they saw."],["UFOs in culture","Flying saucers appeared in countless films, from The Day the Earth Stood Still (1951) to E.T. (1982), and in TV series such as The X-Files. The town of Roswell now has a UFO museum and an annual festival."]],
sp:["The Drake equation","In 1961, the astronomer Frank Drake wrote an equation that multiplies factors such as the number of stars, the share with planets and the chance that life develops, to estimate how many civilizations in our galaxy might be able to communicate. Many of the numbers are still unknown, so estimates range from none to millions."],
g:[["UFO","An unidentified flying object"],["UAP","Unidentified anomalous phenomena, the newer official term"],["SETI","The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence"],["Extraterrestrial","From beyond the Earth"]],
f:["The planet Venus is one of the most common causes of UFO reports.","‘Flying saucer’ came from a newspaper headline, not the pilot’s exact words.","Most UFO reports are solved once enough information is gathered."],
fun:"Roswell, New Mexico, celebrates its UFO fame with streetlights shaped like alien heads.",
tl:"Ang UFO ay anumang bagay sa langit na hindi agad makilala. Karamihan ay napapatunayang eroplano, lobo, satelayt o planeta, at wala pang kumpirmadong ebidensya na alinman dito ay sasakyang pangkalawakan ng mga alien."});

ADDART({id:"propaganda",t:"Propaganda",c:"Society",i:"📢",w:"Propaganda",r:["misinformation","journalism","censorship","hitler","ww2","coldwar"],
p:[["Persuasion on a mass scale","Propaganda is information spread to influence what people think and do."],["War and politics","It was used heavily in the world wars and the Cold War."],["Spotting it","Learning its tricks helps people judge what they see and hear."]],
l:"Propaganda is communication designed to influence people’s opinions and actions, usually for a political cause, by appealing to emotions more than reason and by presenting information selectively or falsely. The word comes from the Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith, set up by the Catholic Church in 1622 to spread Christianity, and at first had no negative meaning. In the 20th century, governments used posters, newspapers, radio and film to rally support for war, and dictatorships such as Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union created ministries to control every message. Propaganda uses techniques such as repetition, slogans, scapegoats, fear, flattering images of leaders and ‘us versus them’ thinking. Today it spreads through television, social media, paid influencers and networks of fake accounts. Not all persuasion is propaganda; the difference often lies in honesty, openness about who is behind the message and respect for people’s ability to decide.",
s:[["Origins","Rulers have always tried to shape opinion, from Egyptian pharaohs’ victory carvings to Roman coins showing emperors. The printing press allowed pamphlets during the Reformation in the 1500s. The modern meaning took hold during World War I, when governments ran large campaigns of posters, films and news to recruit soldiers and portray the enemy as monstrous."],["Dictatorships","In 1933, Nazi Germany created a Ministry of Public Enlightenment and Propaganda under Joseph Goebbels, controlling newspapers, radio, film and art. Cheap radios were produced so every home could hear Hitler’s speeches. The Soviet Union used posters, newspapers such as Pravda and the cult of personality around Stalin. North Korea’s state media still glorifies its leaders."],["Cold War","During the Cold War, both sides broadcast across borders. Radio Free Europe and Voice of America sent news and Western culture into communist countries, while Soviet radio broadcast its own views abroad. Each side portrayed the other as a threat to peace and freedom."],["Propaganda online","Social media lets propaganda spread quickly and target people precisely. Investigators have found ‘troll farms’ that use fake accounts to influence elections and debates, as well as bots that make ideas seem more popular than they are. Governments, platforms and fact-checkers try to detect such campaigns, but people disagree about how far to limit online speech."]],
k:[["Word origin","Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith, 1622"],["Nazi propaganda minister","Joseph Goebbels"],["Common tools","Posters, radio, film, social media"],["Techniques","Repetition, slogans, scapegoats, fear"],["Defense","Media literacy and checking sources"]],
d:[["1622","Catholic Church founds the Congregation for the Propagation of the Faith"],["1914","Kitchener recruiting poster appears in Britain"],["1917","‘I Want You’ Uncle Sam poster in the USA"],["1928","Edward Bernays publishes Propaganda"],["1933","Goebbels’s propaganda ministry created"],["1950","Radio Free Europe begins broadcasting"],["2010s","Troll farms and bots spread online"]],
s2:[["Seven classic techniques","In 1937 the American Institute for Propaganda Analysis listed seven techniques: name-calling, glittering generalities (vague, positive words), transfer (linking to respected symbols), testimonial (celebrity support), plain folks (pretending to be ordinary), card stacking (showing only one side) and the bandwagon (‘everyone is doing it’)."],["Propaganda and advertising","Edward Bernays, a nephew of Sigmund Freud, used psychology to sell products and called his work ‘public relations’. Advertising and political campaigns use some of the same techniques as propaganda, which is why critics say people should look at who is paying for a message."]],
sp:["‘I Want You’","James Montgomery Flagg’s 1917 poster of Uncle Sam pointing at the viewer was based on a 1914 British poster of Lord Kitchener. More than four million copies were printed during World War I, and it was used again in World War II. Flagg used his own face as the model for Uncle Sam."],
g:[["Propaganda","Information used to promote a cause, often misleading"],["Scapegoat","A person or group unfairly blamed for problems"],["Media literacy","The ability to judge and understand media messages"],["Bot","An automated account that posts messages"]],
f:["The word propaganda first had a religious meaning.","The famous Uncle Sam poster was copied from a British design.","Nazi Germany made cheap radios so that more people would hear its broadcasts."],
fun:"The artist of the ‘I Want You’ poster used his own face for Uncle Sam to save the cost of a model.",
tl:"Ang propaganda ay impormasyong ipinakakalat upang impluwensiyahan ang iniisip at ginagawa ng tao, kadalasang umaapela sa damdamin. Malawakan itong ginamit sa mga digmaang pandaigdig at Cold War, at kumakalat na ito ngayon sa social media."});

ADDART({id:"censorship",t:"Censorship",c:"Society",i:"🚫",w:"Censorship",r:["journalism","hrights","propaganda","print","libraries","tiananmen"],
p:[["Controlling information","Censorship is the suppression of speech, writing or images considered unacceptable."],["Burning books","Governments and religious authorities have banned and burned books for centuries."],["Free speech debates","Societies still argue about what, if anything, should be restricted."]],
l:"Censorship is the suppression or control of speech, writing, images or other information by those in authority, usually because it is considered politically dangerous, offensive, immoral or a threat to security. Censors have included kings, churches, governments, armies and, increasingly, private companies. Famous examples include the burning of books in ancient China in 213 BC, the Catholic Church’s Index of Forbidden Books, which lasted from 1559 to 1966, and the Nazi book burnings of 1933. Today, some governments block websites and social media, jail journalists and control television, while democracies generally protect freedom of expression in their constitutions and in Article 19 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Even in free societies, there are debates over limits on hate speech, misinformation, violent material and content shown to children, and over how much power technology companies should have to remove posts.",
s:[["Ancient and religious censorship","In 399 BC, the philosopher Socrates was sentenced to death in Athens partly for corrupting the young with his ideas. Chinese emperor Qin Shi Huang ordered books burned in 213 BC. The Catholic Church’s Index of Forbidden Books banned works by writers including Galileo, Descartes and Victor Hugo until it was abolished in 1966."],["The fight for free speech","In 1644, John Milton’s Areopagitica argued against licensing printers. Britain ended pre-publication licensing in 1695, and the First Amendment to the US Constitution in 1791 protected freedom of speech and the press. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights of 1948 declares that everyone has the right to seek, receive and share information."],["Censorship by dictatorships","On 10 May 1933, Nazi students burned tens of thousands of books by Jewish and left-wing authors in German cities. The Soviet Union required official approval for publications, so writers circulated banned works secretly as typed copies called samizdat. Today, China’s ‘Great Firewall’ blocks many foreign websites, and press freedom groups track journalists imprisoned around the world."],["Modern debates","Schools and libraries debate which books are suitable for children, and challenges to books in some places have risen sharply. Online platforms remove content that breaks their rules, which some see as necessary protection and others as private censorship. Film and game ratings, which advise rather than ban, are a common compromise."]],
k:[["Meaning","Suppressing information or expression"],["Index of Forbidden Books","1559–1966"],["Nazi book burnings","10 May 1933"],["Free speech in UDHR","Article 19 (1948)"],["Famous novel","Fahrenheit 451 by Ray Bradbury (1953)"],["Underground publishing","Samizdat (Soviet Union)"]],
d:[["399 BC","Socrates is sentenced to death"],["213 BC","Qin Shi Huang orders books burned"],["1559","Catholic Church publishes the Index of Forbidden Books"],["1644","Milton’s Areopagitica argues for a free press"],["1791","US First Amendment protects free speech"],["1933","Nazi book burnings"],["1966","The Index is abolished"]],
s2:[["Fahrenheit 451","Ray Bradbury’s 1953 novel imagines a future where firefighters burn books, and 451 degrees Fahrenheit is said to be the temperature at which paper catches fire. Ironically, the book itself has been challenged in some schools."],["Self-censorship","When people fear punishment, losing their job or online abuse, they may avoid saying what they think. This self-censorship can be as powerful as official bans because it is invisible and hard to measure."]],
sp:["Banned Books Week","Since 1982, libraries and bookshops in the United States have held Banned Books Week each year to celebrate the freedom to read. Displays show books that have been challenged, from classics such as To Kill a Mockingbird to the Harry Potter series."],
g:[["Censor","A person or authority that examines and suppresses material"],["Freedom of expression","The right to share ideas and opinions"],["Samizdat","Secretly copied banned writing in the Soviet Union"],["Content moderation","Reviewing and removing posts on online platforms"]],
f:["The Catholic Index of Forbidden Books lasted more than 400 years.","Fahrenheit 451 is about censorship and has itself been challenged.","Article 19 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights protects free expression."],
fun:"Soviet citizens joked that samizdat meant ‘I write it myself, edit it myself, censor it myself, publish it myself, distribute it myself and go to jail for it myself.’",
tl:"Ang sensura ay pagpigil o pagkontrol sa pananalita, pagsulat o larawan ng mga may kapangyarihan. Ipinagbawal at sinunog ang mga aklat sa loob ng maraming siglo, at hanggang ngayon ay pinagtatalunan kung ano ang dapat limitahan."});

ADDART({id:"surveillance",t:"Mass Surveillance",c:"The Modern World",i:"📹",w:"Mass surveillance",r:["dataprivacy","cybersecurity","digitalid","police","censorship","darkweb"],
p:[["Watching everyone","Mass surveillance is the monitoring of whole populations, not just suspects."],["Cameras and data","It uses cameras, phone records, internet data and facial recognition."],["Security versus privacy","Societies debate how much watching is needed for safety."]],
l:"Mass surveillance is the large-scale monitoring of a population by governments or companies, rather than the targeted watching of individual suspects. It includes networks of security cameras, the collection of phone and internet records, facial recognition, tracking of vehicles and phones, and the analysis of huge amounts of data by computers. Governments say surveillance helps prevent terrorism and crime and find missing people. Critics warn that it threatens privacy, discourages free speech and can be abused to target journalists, minorities and political opponents. The East German Stasi built one of the most extensive surveillance systems in history using informers and files, and in 2013 the whistleblower Edward Snowden revealed that US and allied intelligence agencies were collecting enormous amounts of the world’s communications. Laws such as the European Union’s data protection rules try to set limits, while new technologies make watching easier than ever.",
s:[["The idea of being watched","In 1791 the English philosopher Jeremy Bentham designed the Panopticon, a prison where a single guard could watch every cell without being seen, so prisoners would behave as if always observed. George Orwell’s 1949 novel Nineteen Eighty-Four, with its slogan ‘Big Brother is watching you’, made surveillance a symbol of tyranny."],["The Stasi","East Germany’s Ministry for State Security, the Stasi, had about 91,000 employees and nearly 190,000 informers by 1989. It opened letters, bugged homes and kept files on millions of people. After the fall of the Berlin Wall, citizens stormed its offices, and from 1992 people could read their own files, which together stretch about 111 kilometers."],["The Snowden revelations","In June 2013, Edward Snowden, a contractor for the US National Security Agency, gave journalists documents showing programs that collected phone records of millions of Americans and data from internet companies. The revelations caused international outrage, led to some legal reforms and encouraged the spread of encryption. Snowden fled and was given asylum in Russia."],["Cameras and faces","There are estimated to be over a billion surveillance cameras worldwide, many in China. Facial recognition can identify people in crowds, but studies have found it can be less accurate for some groups. The EU’s AI Act, which came into force in 2024, bans some uses such as real-time facial recognition by police in public places except in serious cases."]],
k:[["Panopticon designed","1791, Jeremy Bentham"],["Novel","Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949)"],["Stasi files","About 111 km of records"],["Snowden revelations","June 2013"],["EU data law","GDPR, in force May 2018"]],
d:[["1791","Bentham designs the Panopticon"],["1949","Orwell publishes Nineteen Eighty-Four"],["1950","East Germany creates the Stasi"],["1990","The Stasi is dissolved"],["2013","Snowden reveals mass surveillance programs"],["2018","EU’s GDPR comes into force"],["2024","EU AI Act limits facial recognition"]],
s2:[["Surveillance capitalism","The writer Shoshana Zuboff used the term surveillance capitalism for the business model of companies that collect data about users’ behavior to predict and influence what they will do, often to sell advertising. Supporters say this pays for free services; critics say people do not understand what they are giving away."],["Living with cameras","Studies of camera networks show they can help solve crimes after they happen but are less clear at preventing them. Some cities have banned police use of facial recognition, while others have expanded it."]],
sp:["Big Brother","In Nineteen Eighty-Four, telescreens in every room watch citizens of the state of Oceania, ruled by the Party and its leader Big Brother. The word ‘Orwellian’ is now used for government policies that seem to control people through surveillance and propaganda."],
g:[["Surveillance","Close watching of people or places"],["Informer","A person who secretly gives information to authorities"],["Facial recognition","Software that identifies people from their faces"],["Encryption","Scrambling data so others cannot read it"]],
f:["The Stasi’s files would stretch over 100 kilometers if laid end to end.","Orwell’s Big Brother gave its name to a reality TV show.","Bentham’s Panopticon was never fully built as he designed it."],
fun:"Jeremy Bentham’s preserved skeleton, dressed in his clothes, sits in a glass case at University College London, as he requested.",
tl:"Ang mass surveillance ay malawakang pagbabantay sa buong populasyon gamit ang mga kamera, rekord ng telepono at internet, at facial recognition. Sinasabi ng mga pamahalaan na nakatutulong ito sa seguridad, ngunit nagbababala ang mga kritiko na banta ito sa privacy at kalayaan."});

ADDART({id:"deathpenalty",t:"The Death Penalty",c:"Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Capital punishment",r:["law","hrights","police","un","democ"],
p:[["Execution by the state","The death penalty is the killing of a person by the state as punishment for a crime."],["Abolished by many","More than two-thirds of countries have abolished it in law or practice."],["A deep debate","People disagree about whether it deters crime and whether it is ever just."]],
l:"The death penalty, or capital punishment, is the execution of a person by the state as punishment for a crime. It has been used since ancient times; the Code of Hammurabi, written around 1754 BC, listed many crimes punishable by death. In the 18th century, thinkers such as Cesare Beccaria began arguing that it was cruel and ineffective, and over the past century and a half a growing number of countries have abolished it. According to Amnesty International, 113 countries had abolished it for all crimes by the end of 2025, and 145 had abolished it in law or in practice. Amnesty recorded at least 2,707 executions in 17 countries in 2025, the highest number in decades, most of them in Iran and Saudi Arabia, while China, believed to execute thousands each year, keeps its figures secret. Supporters argue it gives justice for terrible crimes; opponents point to wrongful convictions and human rights.",
s:[["History","In many ancient and medieval societies, death was the punishment for crimes from murder and treason to theft. Executions were often public spectacles. In 1764, the Italian Cesare Beccaria’s book On Crimes and Punishments argued that certain, moderate punishment deters crime better than cruelty. Venezuela, in 1863, became one of the first countries to abolish the death penalty for all crimes."],["Around the world today","Europe has abolished the death penalty almost entirely; it is banned in the European Union, and Belarus is the only European country still carrying out executions. Many countries in Africa and the Americas have abolished it, while it remains in parts of Asia and the Middle East and in the United States, where it is legal in some states and at the federal level. The UN General Assembly has repeatedly called for a worldwide moratorium since 2007."],["Arguments for","Supporters argue that the worst crimes, such as murder and terrorism, deserve the ultimate punishment, that it brings a sense of justice to victims’ families, that it may deter some crimes and that it ensures dangerous criminals can never harm anyone again."],["Arguments against","Opponents argue that innocent people are sometimes executed and mistakes cannot be undone; in the United States, about 200 people sentenced to death have later been exonerated since 1973. They say studies have not shown it deters crime more than long prison sentences, that it is applied unequally against the poor and minorities, and that it violates the right to life."]],
k:[["Also called","Capital punishment"],["Abolished for all crimes","113 countries (end of 2025)"],["Abolished in law or practice","145 countries"],["Recorded executions, 2025","At least 2,707 in 17 countries (excluding China)"],["World Day Against the Death Penalty","10 October"]],
d:[["c. 1754 BC","Code of Hammurabi lists capital crimes"],["1764","Beccaria argues against the death penalty"],["1863","Venezuela abolishes it for all crimes"],["1972","US Supreme Court halts executions"],["1976","US executions resume"],["2007","UN calls for a moratorium"],["2025","Executions reach their highest recorded level in decades"]],
s2:[["Wrongful convictions","DNA evidence, first used in court in the late 1980s, has helped prove the innocence of many people on death row. Groups such as the Innocence Project work to free wrongly convicted prisoners. Such cases are among the strongest arguments used by abolitionists."],["Public opinion","Support for the death penalty varies widely between countries and over time, and often falls when people are offered life imprisonment without parole as an alternative. Some countries abolished it even though most voters supported it, believing it to be a human rights question."]],
sp:["Abolition timeline","In 1977 only 16 countries had abolished the death penalty for all crimes. By the end of 2025 the number had grown to 113. Amnesty International counts a country as abolitionist in practice if it has the penalty in law but has not carried out an execution for at least ten years."],
g:[["Capital punishment","Execution as a legal punishment"],["Abolition","Officially ending something"],["Moratorium","A temporary halt"],["Exonerate","To clear someone of blame or guilt"]],
f:["More than two-thirds of the world’s countries have abolished the death penalty in law or practice.","Belarus is the only country in Europe that still carries out executions.","China’s execution figures are a state secret."],
fun:"The word ‘capital’ in capital punishment comes from the Latin caput, meaning head.",
tl:"Ang parusang kamatayan ay pagpatay ng estado sa isang tao bilang parusa sa krimen. Inalis na ito ng mahigit dalawang-katlo ng mga bansa sa batas o sa praktika, ngunit pinagtatalunan pa rin kung nakapipigil ito sa krimen at kung makatarungan ba ito."});

ADDART({id:"abortion",t:"The Abortion Debate",c:"Society",i:"⚖️",w:"Abortion debate",r:["hrights","law","relig","feminism","democ"],
p:[["Ending a pregnancy","Abortion is the deliberate ending of a pregnancy."],["Deeply divided views","People disagree strongly about when and whether it should be allowed."],["Laws vary","Laws range from full bans to allowing it on request."]],
l:"Abortion is the deliberate ending of a pregnancy so that it does not result in the birth of a child. It is one of the most hotly debated moral and political questions in many countries. People who call themselves ‘pro-life’ generally believe that human life begins at conception and that the unborn have a right to life that the law should protect, often allowing exceptions only in limited cases. People who call themselves ‘pro-choice’ generally believe that a pregnant woman should have the right to decide whether to continue a pregnancy, especially in its early stages, and that legal access makes it safer. Many people hold views in between, depending on the stage of pregnancy and the circumstances. Laws differ widely around the world, from complete bans to allowing abortion on request, and they have changed repeatedly through court rulings, referendums and new legislation, including in the United States, Ireland, Argentina and France in recent years.",
s:[["Moral questions","The debate often turns on questions on which people’s deepest beliefs differ: when a developing human being should be considered a person with rights, how to weigh those rights against the rights and health of the woman, and what role religion and the state should play. Many religious traditions, including the Catholic Church, oppose abortion, while views vary within and among other faiths."],["Health","The World Health Organization estimates that about 73 million induced abortions take place worldwide each year. It says that unsafe abortions, performed without trained help or in unhygienic conditions, cause many deaths and injuries, and supporters of legal access cite this. Opponents argue that support for pregnant women and adoption are better answers."],["Changing laws","In the United States, the Supreme Court’s 1973 decision Roe v. Wade made abortion a constitutional right nationwide; in June 2022, Dobbs v. Jackson overturned Roe, returning the question to individual states, which now have very different laws. Ireland voted in a 2018 referendum to remove its constitutional ban, Argentina legalized abortion in 2020, and France made the freedom to have an abortion part of its constitution in 2024. Some other countries have tightened restrictions."],["How the debate is conducted","Supporters of both sides hold marches, lobby lawmakers and campaign in elections. Each side uses language that reflects its view, such as ‘unborn child’ or ‘fetus’, and ‘pro-life’ or ‘anti-abortion’, ‘pro-choice’ or ‘abortion rights’. Many people find the topic personal and painful, and respectful discussion is often difficult."]],
k:[["Main positions","Pro-life (anti-abortion) and pro-choice (abortion rights)"],["Estimated abortions per year","About 73 million worldwide (WHO)"],["Roe v. Wade","1973, overturned 2022"],["Ireland referendum","2018"],["France constitution change","2024"]],
d:[["1920","Soviet Russia becomes the first country to legalize abortion"],["1967","Britain’s Abortion Act passes"],["1973","Roe v. Wade decided in the USA"],["2018","Ireland votes to remove its constitutional ban"],["2020","Argentina legalizes abortion"],["2022","Dobbs decision overturns Roe v. Wade"],["2024","France adds abortion freedom to its constitution"]],
s2:[["Middle-ground views","Surveys in many countries find that large numbers of people do not fully agree with either side. Some support allowing abortion early in pregnancy but not later; others oppose it in general but accept exceptions, for example when the woman’s life is in danger."],["Medical changes","Since the late 1980s, medicines have made it possible to end early pregnancies without surgery. Their availability, especially by mail, has become a new area of legal dispute in places where abortion is restricted."]],
sp:["Words in the debate","Both sides choose words carefully because words frame the issue. Neutral reference works and many news organizations try to use terms such as ‘abortion opponents’ and ‘abortion rights supporters’ to describe the two sides fairly."],
g:[["Pregnancy","The time when a baby develops inside the mother’s womb"],["Fetus","An unborn offspring in the later stages of development"],["Referendum","A vote by the public on a single question"],["Constitution","A country’s basic law"]],
f:["Laws on abortion vary from complete bans to allowing it on request.","France was the first country to name abortion freedom in its constitution.","In the United States, abortion law is now decided mainly by each state."],
fun:"Both sides of the debate use the language of rights: the right to life and the right to choose.",
tl:"Ang aborsyon ay sinadyang pagwawakas ng pagbubuntis. Mahigpit itong pinagtatalunan: naniniwala ang mga ‘pro-life’ na dapat protektahan ang buhay ng hindi pa isinisilang, habang naniniwala ang mga ‘pro-choice’ na dapat ang babae ang magpasya. Iba-iba ang batas sa bawat bansa."});

ADDART({id:"cults",t:"Cults and High-Control Groups",c:"Society",i:"🔒",w:"Cult",r:["relig","conspiracy","secretsocieties","propaganda","terrorism"],
p:[["Total control","A cult is a group that uses manipulation to control its members’ lives."],["Charismatic leaders","Most center on a leader who claims special authority."],["Tragic endings","Some, such as Jonestown, ended in mass death."]],
l:"‘Cult’ is a term commonly used for a group, often religious but sometimes political or commercial, that is devoted to a charismatic leader and uses manipulation and pressure to control its members’ thoughts, relationships and daily lives. Because the word is used loosely and can unfairly insult new or unusual faiths, many scholars prefer neutral terms such as ‘new religious movement’, and use ‘high-control group’ for those that are abusive. Warning signs described by psychologists include demanding total obedience to a leader, cutting members off from family and friends, controlling information, using fear and guilt, and making it very hard to leave. A few such groups have ended in tragedy, including the Peoples Temple at Jonestown, where more than 900 people died in 1978, and Aum Shinrikyo, which released poison gas on the Tokyo subway in 1995. Former members often need time and support to rebuild their lives.",
s:[["How control works","The psychiatrist Robert Jay Lifton studied ‘thought reform’ in the 1950s and described methods such as controlling all communication, demanding confession, using special language that limits thinking and teaching that the group’s doctrine is more important than any individual. Recruits are often welcomed with intense friendliness, sometimes called love bombing, before demands increase."],["Jonestown","Jim Jones founded the Peoples Temple in the United States, and in the 1970s moved many members to a settlement in the jungle of Guyana. On 18 November 1978, after followers killed a visiting US congressman and others, Jones led a mass murder–suicide in which 918 people died, including about 300 children, most by drinking poisoned fruit drink."],["Other tragedies","In 1993 a 51-day standoff between US federal agents and the Branch Davidians near Waco, Texas, ended in a fire in which 76 people died. On 20 March 1995 members of Aum Shinrikyo released sarin nerve gas on the Tokyo subway, killing 13 people and injuring thousands. In 1997, 39 members of Heaven’s Gate in California died believing they would join a spacecraft behind a comet."],["Leaving and recovering","People leave high-control groups for many reasons, including doubts, abuse or contact with outsiders. They may struggle with lost years, broken family ties and fear. Support groups, counseling and former members’ stories help them recover. Experts warn against forced ‘deprogramming’, which was once used and caused its own harm."]],
k:[["Neutral scholarly term","New religious movement"],["Common features","Charismatic leader, isolation, control of information"],["Jonestown deaths","918, on 18 November 1978"],["Tokyo subway attack","20 March 1995"],["Psychologist","Robert Jay Lifton, thought reform"]],
d:[["1961","Lifton publishes his study of thought reform"],["1978","Jonestown deaths in Guyana"],["1993","Waco siege ends in fire"],["1995","Aum Shinrikyo sarin attack in Tokyo"],["1997","Heaven’s Gate deaths in California"],["2018","Aum Shinrikyo leader is executed in Japan"]],
s2:[["Why smart people join","Research shows that people who join high-control groups are usually not unintelligent or unusual. Many join at a time of loneliness, loss or change, attracted by friendship, purpose and simple answers. Groups reveal their more extreme beliefs only gradually."],["Freedom of religion","Democracies protect freedom of religion, so they generally act against groups only when laws are broken, such as through abuse, fraud or violence. Deciding when an unusual faith becomes a dangerous group is often difficult and controversial."]],
sp:["‘Drinking the Kool-Aid’","The English phrase ‘drinking the Kool-Aid’, meaning blindly following a group, comes from Jonestown, although the drink used was mostly a similar product called Flavor Aid. Many survivors and relatives find the phrase hurtful because it makes light of a tragedy in which many were forced or deceived."],
g:[["Charismatic","Having a powerful personal charm that attracts followers"],["Manipulation","Influencing someone unfairly or dishonestly"],["Isolation","Being cut off from others"],["New religious movement","A recently founded religious group"]],
f:["The Jonestown tragedy was the largest loss of American civilian life in a single deliberate act until 11 September 2001.","Scholars often avoid the word ‘cult’ because it can be used as an insult.","Many recruits are drawn in by friendship, not beliefs."],
fun:"The word cult originally simply meant worship, from the Latin cultus, and is still used that way in phrases like ‘the cult of saints’.",
tl:"Ang kulto ay grupong nakasentro sa isang karismatikong lider at gumagamit ng manipulasyon upang kontrolin ang buhay ng mga kasapi. Humantong ang ilan sa trahedya, gaya ng Jonestown noong 1978, kung saan mahigit 900 ang namatay."});

ADDART({id:"organizedcrime",t:"Organized Crime",c:"Society",i:"🕴️",w:"Organized crime",r:["police","law","drugtrade","trafficking","italycountry"],
p:[["Crime as a business","Organized crime groups run illegal businesses for profit."],["Mafia, cartels and yakuza","Famous examples include the Sicilian Mafia, Mexican cartels and Japanese yakuza."],["Fighting back","Police, courts and brave citizens have taken on powerful gangs."]],
l:"Organized crime is criminal activity carried out by structured groups that work, like businesses, over long periods to make money through illegal means. Their activities include drug trafficking, extortion, human trafficking, illegal gambling, smuggling, fraud, money laundering and corruption of officials, often backed by violence or threats. Well-known groups include the Sicilian Mafia, or Cosa Nostra, and other Italian groups such as the ’Ndrangheta and Camorra; the American Mafia; drug cartels in Mexico and Colombia; the Japanese yakuza; Chinese triads; and Russian and Balkan gangs. Many have codes of loyalty and silence. Organized crime corrupts governments, harms economies and causes many deaths, so countries cooperate to fight it through special laws, witness protection, financial investigations and international treaties such as the United Nations Convention against Transnational Organized Crime of 2000.",
s:[["The Mafia","The Sicilian Mafia emerged in the 19th century, when landowners hired armed men to protect estates. Its members follow omertà, a code of silence. Italian immigrants brought the Mafia to the United States, where Prohibition (1920–33), the ban on alcohol, made gangsters such as Al Capone rich from illegal liquor. Capone was finally jailed in 1931, not for violence but for tax evasion."],["Fighting the Mafia","In Italy’s Maxi Trial of 1986–87, held in a fortified courtroom in Palermo, 338 Mafia members were convicted, thanks to the work of prosecutors Giovanni Falcone and Paolo Borsellino and the testimony of informers. In 1992 the Mafia murdered both men with bombs, causing national outrage and a crackdown. Matteo Messina Denaro, a boss who had been on the run for 30 years, was arrested in 2023."],["Cartels and other groups","Drug cartels in Colombia, such as Pablo Escobar’s Medellín cartel, grew rich in the 1980s from cocaine. Mexican cartels later took over much of the trade and have fought violent wars with each other and with the government. Japan’s yakuza once operated fairly openly with offices and badges, but anti-gang laws since 1992 have reduced their numbers."],["Tools against organized crime","The US RICO Act of 1970 lets prosecutors charge leaders for crimes committed by their organizations. Tracking money through banks, seizing criminals’ property, protecting witnesses and international cooperation through bodies such as Europol and Interpol are key strategies. Corruption and poverty make organized crime harder to defeat."]],
k:[["Famous groups","Sicilian Mafia, ’Ndrangheta, cartels, yakuza, triads"],["Code of silence","Omertà"],["Maxi Trial","1986–87, Palermo, 338 convicted"],["US law","RICO Act (1970)"],["UN treaty","Palermo Convention (2000)"]],
d:[["1920–33","Prohibition fuels gangsters in the USA"],["1931","Al Capone convicted of tax evasion"],["1970","US passes the RICO Act"],["1986–87","Maxi Trial in Palermo"],["1992","Falcone and Borsellino murdered; Japan passes anti-yakuza law"],["1993","Pablo Escobar killed in Colombia"],["2000","UN Convention against Transnational Organized Crime"],["2023","Matteo Messina Denaro arrested"]],
s2:[["Money laundering","Criminals need to make illegal money look legal, which is called money laundering. They may pass it through shops, property, casinos or shell companies. Banks must report suspicious transactions, and investigators ‘follow the money’ to find gang leaders."],["Mafia in films","Films such as The Godfather (1972) made the Mafia famous worldwide, sometimes glamorizing it. Many Italians, and the families of victims, stress that the real Mafia is a source of fear, violence and poverty."]],
sp:["Addiopizzo","In 2004, young people in Palermo covered the city with stickers saying ‘An entire people who pay the pizzo is a people without dignity’. The pizzo is protection money demanded by the Mafia. Their Addiopizzo movement now lists hundreds of businesses that refuse to pay."],
g:[["Extortion","Getting money by threats"],["Money laundering","Making illegal money appear legal"],["Omertà","The Mafia’s code of silence"],["Cartel","A group, often of drug traffickers, that controls a trade"]],
f:["Al Capone was jailed for not paying taxes.","The Maxi Trial courtroom in Palermo was built to withstand rocket attacks.","Palermo’s airport is named after Falcone and Borsellino."],
fun:"Yakuza members traditionally covered their bodies in large tattoos, so many Japanese hot springs still ban tattooed visitors.",
tl:"Ang organisadong krimen ay mga grupong kriminal na tumatakbo na parang negosyo, tulad ng Mafia, mga kartel ng droga at yakuza. Kumikita sila sa droga, pangingikil at korapsyon, kaya nagtutulungan ang mga bansa upang labanan sila."});

ADDART({id:"drugtrade",t:"The Illegal Drug Trade",c:"Society",i:"💊",w:"Illegal drug trade",r:["organizedcrime","phdrugwar","mental","law","police"],
p:[["A global business","The illegal drug trade grows, smuggles and sells banned substances."],["The war on drugs","Many governments have fought drugs with police and prisons."],["Different approaches","Some countries now treat drug use mainly as a health issue."]],
l:"The illegal drug trade is the global business of growing, making, smuggling and selling drugs that are banned by law, such as cocaine, heroin, methamphetamine, fentanyl and, in many countries, cannabis. It is worth hundreds of billions of dollars a year and is controlled largely by organized crime. The United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime estimates that over 300 million people use illegal drugs each year. Drugs can cause addiction, illness and overdose deaths, while the trade fuels violence, corruption and the spread of weapons. International treaties since 1912 have banned many drugs, and from the 1970s the United States led a ‘war on drugs’ focused on arrests and destroying supplies. Critics say this approach filled prisons and caused bloodshed without reducing drug use, and some countries, such as Portugal, have shifted towards treatment, while others, such as Canada and Uruguay, have legalized cannabis. Others keep strict laws and harsh penalties.",
s:[["History","The British fought the Opium Wars against China in 1839–42 and 1856–60 partly to keep selling opium there. The International Opium Convention of 1912 began global drug control, and the UN’s Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs of 1961 remains its basis. In 1971 US President Richard Nixon called drug abuse ‘public enemy number one’, and the phrase ‘war on drugs’ became popular."],["Trafficking routes","Most cocaine comes from coca grown in Colombia, Peru and Bolivia and is smuggled to North America and Europe. Most of the world’s heroin once came from opium grown in Afghanistan and the ‘Golden Triangle’ of Myanmar, Laos and Thailand. Synthetic drugs such as methamphetamine and fentanyl can be made in labs from chemicals, making them harder to stop."],["The opioid crisis","In the United States, the overprescription of opioid painkillers from the 1990s and the arrival of the extremely powerful synthetic opioid fentanyl caused a crisis in which yearly overdose deaths rose above 100,000 around 2022–23, before beginning to fall in 2024. Lawsuits against drug companies have resulted in billions of dollars in settlements."],["Approaches to drug policy","Strict approaches focus on punishment, border control and destroying crops; some countries in Asia and the Middle East impose the death penalty for trafficking. Harm-reduction approaches, such as treatment programs, clean needles and overdose-reversing medicine, aim to keep people alive and healthy. In 2001 Portugal decriminalized personal possession of all drugs, treating users through health services; supporters and critics still debate the results."]],
k:[["Users worldwide","Over 300 million a year (UNODC)"],["Main UN treaty","Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs (1961)"],["‘War on drugs’","Phrase popularized from 1971"],["Decriminalization pioneer","Portugal, 2001"],["First country to legalize cannabis","Uruguay, 2013"]],
d:[["1839–42","First Opium War"],["1912","International Opium Convention"],["1961","UN Single Convention on Narcotic Drugs"],["1971","Nixon declares drug abuse ‘public enemy number one’"],["1993","Pablo Escobar is killed"],["2001","Portugal decriminalizes drug possession"],["2013","Uruguay legalizes cannabis"],["2018","Canada legalizes cannabis"]],
s2:[["Why drugs are addictive","Many drugs flood the brain’s reward system with dopamine, a chemical linked to pleasure. Over time, the brain adapts, so a person needs more of the drug to feel normal and suffers withdrawal without it. Addiction is recognized as a medical condition that can be treated, although recovery can take a long time."],["Getting help","Health experts encourage anyone worried about their own or someone else’s drug use to talk to a doctor, school counsellor or trusted adult. Many countries have free, confidential help lines and treatment services."]],
sp:["Pablo Escobar","Pablo Escobar led Colombia’s Medellín cartel, which supplied most of the cocaine reaching the United States in the 1980s and made him one of the richest people in the world. His war against the state killed thousands, including judges, police and a presidential candidate. He was shot dead by police in Medellín on 2 December 1993."],
g:[["Trafficking","Illegally trading goods such as drugs"],["Addiction","A condition in which a person cannot stop using a substance"],["Decriminalize","To stop treating something as a crime"],["Harm reduction","Policies that reduce the damage caused by drug use"]],
f:["The first international drug treaty was signed in 1912.","Fentanyl is far stronger than heroin, so tiny amounts can be deadly.","Uruguay was the first country to fully legalize cannabis."],
fun:"Hippos brought to Colombia for Pablo Escobar’s private zoo escaped after his death, and their descendants now number over 150, becoming a serious environmental problem.",
tl:"Ang ilegal na kalakalan ng droga ay pandaigdigang negosyo ng paggawa, pagpupuslit at pagbebenta ng mga bawal na gamot, na kontrolado ng organisadong krimen. Ginamit ng maraming bansa ang ‘war on drugs’, habang tinitingnan na ng iba ang paggamit ng droga bilang usaping pangkalusugan."});

ADDART({id:"trafficking",t:"Human Trafficking",c:"Society",i:"⛓️",w:"Human trafficking",r:["slavetrade","hrights","organizedcrime","migration","onlinescams"],
p:[["Modern slavery","Human trafficking is the trade in people for forced labor or exploitation."],["Millions affected","An estimated 50 million people live in modern slavery."],["Fighting it","Laws, aid groups and awareness help rescue victims and punish traffickers."]],
l:"Human trafficking is the recruitment, transport or holding of people by force, fraud or coercion in order to exploit them, for example through forced labor, sexual exploitation, forced marriage, forced begging or the removal of organs. It is often called modern slavery. Victims may be lured by false job offers, debts or promises of a better life, and then have their documents taken, be threatened or kept in debt they can never repay. The International Labour Organization and partners estimated in 2022 that about 50 million people were in modern slavery, including 28 million in forced labor and 22 million in forced marriages. Trafficking happens in every region, in industries such as farming, fishing, construction, domestic work and online scam centers. The United Nations Palermo Protocol of 2000 defines trafficking and requires countries to criminalize it, protect victims and work together to stop it.",
s:[["How trafficking works","Traffickers often target people who are poor, displaced by war or disasters, or desperate for work. Recruiters may be acquaintances or even relatives. Victims can be moved across borders or trafficked within their own country. Many are not physically locked up but are controlled by threats, debt, isolation or fear of the police."],["Forms of exploitation","Forced labor is the most common form, found in factories, mines, fishing boats and private homes. Children are trafficked for labor, begging and armed groups. Since the early 2020s, criminal gangs in parts of Southeast Asia have held hundreds of thousands of people in compounds, forcing them to run online scams."],["Laws and action","The Palermo Protocol, adopted by the UN on 15 November 2000, was the first international agreement with an agreed definition of trafficking. The US Trafficking in Persons Report ranks countries on their efforts each year. Police raids, labor inspections and partnerships with companies to check supply chains aim to stop exploitation."],["Helping victims","Survivors may need safe housing, medical and psychological care, legal help and protection from being punished for crimes they were forced to commit. Awareness campaigns teach people to recognize warning signs, such as a worker who is not allowed to keep their passport or seems controlled by someone else."]],
k:[["Modern slavery (2022 estimate)","About 50 million people"],["Forced labor","About 28 million"],["Forced marriage","About 22 million"],["Key treaty","Palermo Protocol (2000)"],["World Day against Trafficking","30 July"]],
d:[["1926","League of Nations Slavery Convention"],["2000","UN adopts the Palermo Protocol"],["2003","Palermo Protocol comes into force"],["2013","UN declares 30 July World Day against Trafficking in Persons"],["2015","UN goals call for ending modern slavery"],["2022","Global estimate finds 50 million in modern slavery"]],
s2:[["Products and supply chains","Goods such as seafood, cocoa, cotton, electronics and clothing can be linked to forced labor somewhere along their supply chains. Some countries now require large companies to report on what they do to prevent slavery, and consumers can look for certified products."],["Scam compounds","Many people have been lured to countries such as Myanmar and Cambodia with offers of well-paid office jobs, only to be held in guarded compounds and forced to cheat strangers online through fake romances and investments. Governments in the region have carried out raids and rescued thousands."]],
sp:["The blue heart","The Blue Heart Campaign of the United Nations uses a blue heart as a symbol of sorrow for victims of trafficking and the coldness of those who buy and sell people. People wear blue hearts to show support and raise awareness."],
g:[["Exploitation","Unfairly using someone for profit"],["Coercion","Forcing someone through threats or pressure"],["Debt bondage","Being forced to work to pay off a debt that keeps growing"],["Supply chain","All the steps in making and delivering a product"]],
f:["The Palermo Protocol was adopted on 15 November 2000.","Forced labor is the most common form of human trafficking.","Trafficking can happen within a country, not just across borders."],
fun:"Some fishing companies now use satellite tracking to check how long boats stay at sea, because very long voyages can be a warning sign of forced labor.",
tl:"Ang human trafficking ay pagkuha, paglilipat o pagkulong sa mga tao sa pamamagitan ng dahas o panlilinlang upang pagsamantalahan sila, gaya ng sapilitang paggawa. Tinatayang 50 milyong tao ang nasa makabagong pang-aalipin."});

ADDART({id:"hacking",t:"Hackers and Cybercrime",c:"Technology",i:"💻",w:"Security hacker",r:["cybersecurity","internet","comp","darkweb","onlinescams","coding"],
p:[["Breaking into systems","Hackers find ways into computers and networks, for good or bad purposes."],["Cybercrime","Criminals steal data, spread viruses and demand ransoms."],["White hats","Ethical hackers help organizations fix weaknesses before criminals find them."]],
l:"A hacker is someone skilled at exploring and getting around the limits of computer systems. Originally the word described clever programrs at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in the 1960s, but it now usually refers to people who break into computers and networks. ‘White hat’ or ethical hackers test systems with permission to find weaknesses, while ‘black hat’ hackers break in illegally to steal money or data, spy or cause damage; ‘gray hats’ fall in between. Cybercrime includes stealing personal information, breaking into bank accounts, spreading malicious software such as viruses and worms, online fraud and ransomware attacks that lock up a victim’s files until a payment is made. Famous incidents include the Morris worm of 1988, the ILOVEYOU virus of 2000 and the WannaCry ransomware of 2017. Countries have passed cybercrime laws, and many companies pay rewards called bug bounties to people who report security flaws responsibly.",
s:[["Early hackers","At MIT in the late 1950s and 1960s, members of the Tech Model Railroad Club used ‘hack’ to mean a clever solution. In the 1970s, ‘phone phreaks’ found they could control the telephone network with a 2,600-hertz tone, which one produced with a toy whistle from a cereal box. Hacker culture valued curiosity and the free sharing of information."],["Famous attacks","On 2 November 1988, the Morris worm, released by a graduate student, slowed thousands of computers, about a tenth of the internet at the time. On 4 May 2000, the ILOVEYOU virus, sent as an email love letter, spread to millions of computers worldwide; it was traced to a student in Manila, and because the Philippines had no law against it yet, he was never charged. In May 2017, WannaCry ransomware hit over 200,000 computers in 150 countries, including Britain’s National Health Service."],["Cybercrime today","Ransomware gangs attack hospitals, schools, companies and cities, and some operate like businesses with help desks. Many attacks begin with phishing, messages that trick people into giving away passwords. Some hacking is carried out or supported by governments for spying or sabotage; the Stuxnet worm, discovered in 2010, damaged machines in Iran’s nuclear program."],["Defending against hackers","Ethical hackers run ‘penetration tests’ and enter competitions such as capture the flag. The Budapest Convention of 2001 was the first international treaty on cybercrime, and the UN adopted a new cybercrime convention in 2024. Simple habits, such as strong unique passwords, two-factor authentication and updating software, block most attacks."]],
k:[["Ethical hackers","White hats"],["Criminal hackers","Black hats"],["Morris worm","2 November 1988"],["ILOVEYOU virus","4 May 2000"],["WannaCry ransomware","May 2017"],["First cybercrime treaty","Budapest Convention (2001)"]],
d:[["1960s","‘Hacker’ is used at MIT for clever programrs"],["1971","Phone phreaking becomes widely known"],["1988","Morris worm spreads"],["1993","DEF CON hacker conference begins"],["2000","ILOVEYOU virus; Philippines passes its E-Commerce Act"],["2001","Budapest Convention on cybercrime"],["2010","Stuxnet discovered"],["2017","WannaCry ransomware attack"]],
s2:[["Bug bounties","Many companies, from Google to banks, pay ethical hackers who find and report security flaws, sometimes tens of thousands of dollars for serious bugs. Responsible disclosure means telling the company first and giving it time to fix the problem before making it public."],["Social engineering","Many successful attacks trick people rather than machines. Pretending to be a boss, a bank or technical support, attackers persuade victims to reveal passwords or send money. Training staff to pause and check is one of the best defenses."]],
sp:["ILOVEYOU","The ILOVEYOU worm arrived as an email with the subject ‘ILOVEYOU’ and an attachment that looked like a love letter. When opened, it overwrote files and sent itself to everyone in the victim’s address book. Damage was estimated at billions of dollars, and it led the Philippines to pass a law covering computer crimes."],
g:[["Malware","Malicious software, such as viruses and ransomware"],["Ransomware","Malware that locks files until a payment is made"],["Phishing","Tricking people into revealing information"],["Two-factor authentication","Using a second check, such as a code, to log in"]],
f:["The word hacker first meant a clever programr, not a criminal.","The ILOVEYOU virus came from Manila in 2000.","Most hacking attacks begin by tricking a person."],
fun:"Phone phreaks discovered that a free plastic whistle from Cap’n Crunch cereal boxes produced the exact tone needed to control long-distance phone lines.",
tl:"Ang hacker ay taong mahusay pumasok o lumampas sa mga limitasyon ng sistema ng kompyuter. Tumutulong ang mga ‘white hat’ na ayusin ang kahinaan, habang nagnanakaw o nanggugulo ang mga ‘black hat’. Kabilang sa cybercrime ang pagnanakaw ng datos, virus at ransomware."});

ADDART({id:"gambling",t:"Gambling",c:"How We Live",i:"🎲",w:"Gambling",r:["prob","boardgames","organizedcrime","horseracing","financialliteracy"],
p:[["Betting on chance","Gambling means risking money on an uncertain outcome to win more."],["The house always wins","Casinos and lotteries are designed so that, over time, they make money."],["A risky habit","For some people, gambling becomes an addiction."]],
l:"Gambling is betting money or something of value on an event with an uncertain outcome, such as a game of cards, a roll of dice, a lottery draw or a sports match, in the hope of winning more. People have gambled since ancient times; dice have been found in sites thousands of years old. Today gambling takes place in casinos, betting shops, lotteries and, increasingly, online and on phones. Governments raise money by taxing it or running lotteries, while some ban or restrict it. Games of chance helped inspire the mathematics of probability, which explains why the ‘house’ always has an advantage: every casino game is designed so that, on average, players lose a little of what they bet. For most people gambling is occasional entertainment, but for some it becomes a harmful addiction, recognized as a medical disorder, that can lead to debt, broken relationships and crime.",
s:[["History","Dice made from bones and later from clay and ivory were used in ancient Mesopotamia, India, Egypt and Rome. Playing cards spread from China to Europe by the 14th century. The Ridotto in Venice, opened in 1638, is often called the first casino, and Monte Carlo’s casino opened in 1863. Nevada legalized casino gambling in 1931, and Las Vegas grew into a gambling capital."],["Probability and the house edge","In 1654, Blaise Pascal and Pierre de Fermat solved a problem about dividing the stakes of an unfinished game, founding probability theory. In European roulette, with numbers 0 to 36, a bet on one number pays 35 to 1 although the chance is 1 in 37; this gap, the house edge of about 2.7 percent, guarantees that the casino wins over time."],["Gambling today","Online casinos and sports betting have grown rapidly since the 2000s. In 2018 the US Supreme Court allowed states to legalize sports betting, and many did. Critics worry about constant advertising, betting apps that are always available and video games with ‘loot boxes’, paid random rewards that some countries treat as gambling."],["Problem gambling","Gambling disorder was recognized as an addiction in the American Psychiatric Association’s manual in 2013. Signs include betting more to win back losses, hiding gambling and borrowing money to gamble. Help includes counseling, support groups such as Gamblers Anonymous and tools that let people block themselves from betting sites."]],
k:[["First casino","Ridotto, Venice, 1638"],["Probability founded","1654, Pascal and Fermat"],["European roulette house edge","About 2.7%"],["Las Vegas legal gambling","1931"],["Gambling disorder recognized","2013 (DSM-5)"]],
d:[["c. 3000 BC","Early dice used in Mesopotamia"],["1638","The Ridotto opens in Venice"],["1654","Pascal and Fermat found probability theory"],["1863","Monte Carlo casino opens"],["1931","Nevada legalizes gambling"],["2013","Gambling disorder recognized as an addiction"],["2018","US Supreme Court opens the way for sports betting"]],
s2:[["The gambler’s fallacy","Many gamblers believe that after a run of one result, the opposite is ‘due’. In fact, each spin or coin toss is independent. On 18 August 1913, at Monte Carlo, black came up 26 times in a row at roulette, and gamblers lost millions betting that red must come next."],["Lotteries","Lotteries have funded public works for centuries, including parts of the Great Wall of China, according to legend, and early American universities. The chance of winning a big jackpot is usually tiny, often less than one in ten million, which is why economists sometimes call lotteries ‘a tax on people who are bad at maths’."]],
sp:["The Monte Carlo fallacy","The record streak of 26 blacks at Monte Carlo in 1913 gave the gambler’s fallacy its other name, the Monte Carlo fallacy. The chance of such a streak is about 1 in 66 million, but no spin depends on the one before."],
g:[["Odds","The chances of something happening, or the payout ratio of a bet"],["House edge","The casino’s built-in advantage"],["Probability","How likely something is to happen"],["Loot box","A paid, random reward in a video game"]],
f:["The mathematics of probability began with a gambling problem.","Every casino game is designed to favor the casino in the long run.","Gambling disorder is recognized as an addiction, like drug addiction."],
fun:"The numbers on a roulette wheel from 0 to 36 add up to 666, which is why roulette is sometimes nicknamed ‘the devil’s wheel’.",
tl:"Ang pagsusugal ay pagtaya ng pera sa isang pangyayaring hindi tiyak ang resulta upang manalo nang higit pa. Dinisenyo ang mga casino at loterya upang kumita sila sa katagalan, at para sa ilan, nagiging adiksyon ang pagsusugal."});
